WEBVTT Kind: captions; Language: en

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Why did you come back?

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:25.001
I'm wondering.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.001
As soon as I got back I
realized why I had left.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.000
When did you come? Was it two years ago?

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.001
Do you know exactly when?

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.001
Yeah, October. The last time I worked
was October of whenever, two years

00:00:48.001 --> 00:00:50.000
ago. So it's over two years.

00:00:52.001 --> 00:00:53.001
And that was?

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Superman.

00:00:55.001 --> 00:00:59.000
Superman, yeah.
You made a lot of money on that.

00:00:59.001 --> 00:01:00.000
Yeah, I think so.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.001
You must have quit for a couple of years.

00:01:02.001 --> 00:01:02.001
Yeah.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Did that have any...

00:01:04.001 --> 00:01:10.001
Well, it had some very not.
Only in that I didn't particularly

00:01:10.001 --> 00:01:12.000
like what I was doing in the film.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Not that the film was bad, but I just didn't...

00:01:18.001 --> 00:01:21.001
I just felt that I shouldn't be taking
those kind of films anymore for a while.

00:01:21.001 --> 00:01:23.001
So I just decided to stop for a year.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
And then I decided to go back to work.
There just wasn't anything

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
available right away.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
What you wanted to do.
When you said you didn't want to do that, you

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.000
saw the script of course.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:35.001
Superman?

00:01:35.001 --> 00:01:36.000
Yeah.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.001
Oh yeah, it's one of those things where you

00:01:37.001 --> 00:01:39.000
think, yeah, I can make something out of that.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.000
And then you get out there
and then you find yourself

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.001
being involved in a big production.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Just being a little cog in a big wheel.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.001
And it isn't a hell of a lot of fun.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.001
Although it may look like it.
The end product may look like fun.

00:01:58.001 --> 00:02:00.001
You'd be waiting around pricing crazy.

00:02:01.001 --> 00:02:02.000
On a set.

00:02:02.001 --> 00:02:02.001
Yeah.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.000
I mean, five, six hours at a time.
Sometimes between setups.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.000
What do you do?

00:02:09.001 --> 00:02:14.000
Well, I just started to read,
which I've almost never done.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Once I said I always kind of think
about what I'm going to be doing or

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.000
what I'd rather be doing.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Could you have been in the second Superman?

00:02:25.001 --> 00:02:26.000
I wasn't.

00:02:26.001 --> 00:02:28.000
But they did all mine together.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:28.001
Oh, that's good.

00:02:28.001 --> 00:02:33.000
They did. I worked about seven weeks
and they did both one and two.

00:02:34.001 --> 00:02:35.001
At least I think I'm in it. I don't know.

00:02:36.001 --> 00:02:37.001
They may cut it out.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Do you have points in the money?

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.000
No. So they won't have any reason
to cut it out unless it's bad.

00:02:42.001 --> 00:02:43.001
This is also a possibility.

00:02:45.001 --> 00:02:48.000
So from October of seventy...

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:49.001
Seventy?

00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:50.001
Seventy-seven?

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:51.001
Mm-hmm.

00:02:52.001 --> 00:02:53.001
It's almost three years.

00:02:54.001 --> 00:02:55.000
Yeah.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.000
It's close to seven years.

00:02:56.001 --> 00:02:57.001
So what have you been doing?

00:02:58.001 --> 00:03:01.000
I don't know.
I worked two days and one for one.

00:03:01.001 --> 00:03:02.001
That's the only thing I can remember.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.001
Really? What's been happening?

00:03:07.001 --> 00:03:10.001
Well,
we moved up to Pebble Beach for almost a year.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.001
And I haven't really done
anything constructive.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
I read a lot under the
guise that I'm looking for

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.001
movie material, you know?

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Are you your own production company?

00:03:28.001 --> 00:03:31.001
No, really. I don't have a company
that I work out of, but it isn't.

00:03:31.001 --> 00:03:33.000
That's the type of thing.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.001
Yeah. It isn't really.

00:03:35.001 --> 00:03:39.001
And I have a couple of scripts
under development now.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Ideas that I've had with me jointly
with a couple of people and

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.000
we're writing some stories.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.000
I'm not writing them.
I don't have them written.

00:03:51.001 --> 00:03:53.000
So the first year you were in Pebble Beach?

00:03:53.001 --> 00:03:55.000
Is that it?
Right after that you went up to Pebble Beach?

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:57.001
And what did you do every day? Did you just...

00:03:57.001 --> 00:04:00.001
Well, we were working on a house there,
so it kept me busy for a long time.

00:04:00.001 --> 00:04:04.001
We had moved into one house and then
we were trying to develop another

00:04:04.001 --> 00:04:06.000
house down on the beach.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.001
And it was this wonderful old stone house
that had been there about 60 years.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.001
But it had marble floors,
which were just ice cold, you

00:04:15.001 --> 00:04:17.000
know, right next to the beach.

00:04:17.001 --> 00:04:18.000
They were clamming.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.000
So I decided I would take
the marble floors out.

00:04:22.001 --> 00:04:26.001
And it looks so good without the marble
floors that I decided I would take all

00:04:26.001 --> 00:04:29.000
the plaster off the walls and expose the stone.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.001
So one thing led to another
and before long it was

00:04:33.001 --> 00:04:35.001
gutted. It was just totally gutted.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.000
It looked beautiful. I mean,
you can see all this wonderful stone, great big

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:47.001
pieces of... ...this very soft
stone that the sea air had kind of

00:04:47.001 --> 00:04:49.001
eroded on the outside of the house.

00:04:49.001 --> 00:04:51.000
And then the inside was just wonderful.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.000
And then we had plans to put in,
you know, hardwood floors and all that.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Did you do anything yourself, actually?

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.000
I did a lot of the demolition.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:04.001
I was good at that.

00:05:05.001 --> 00:05:08.001
That was good therapy. You get in there
with a sledgehammer and take care of it.

00:05:08.001 --> 00:05:10.000
You didn't get bored that year?

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:11.001
Not really, no.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.001
Red, cool around with the house?

00:05:13.001 --> 00:05:15.001
And I fly a little, you know, kind of stuff.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:16.001
What do you fly?

00:05:16.001 --> 00:05:20.001
What kind of airplanes?

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.001
Oh, I've had a number of airplanes.

00:05:25.001 --> 00:05:28.000
I've had 20s and, you know,
King Airs and I've had

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.001
biplanes, aerobatic stunt planes.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.001
I have a little bonanza right now.

00:05:33.001 --> 00:05:35.000
You checked out on it and it's tremendous.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:37.001
That is a jet?

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.000
No, it's a prop jet.

00:05:39.001 --> 00:05:40.001
A prop jet, yeah.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.000
How many hours do you have in there?

00:05:42.001 --> 00:05:43.000
[...] 1,500.

00:05:43.001 --> 00:05:46.000
I guess you and Chris Reeve had a great time.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.000
Yeah, we did.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.000
About six to eight hours every day on the set.

00:05:49.001 --> 00:05:51.000
We talked about flying a lot.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.000
As a matter of fact, we flew to Italy once.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:57.001
Together.

00:05:58.001 --> 00:06:00.000
Well,
in that picture you didn't have to fly enough.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:01.001
Yeah, right.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.000
You're not into gliders, are you?

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:04.001
No, he is.

00:06:05.001 --> 00:06:08.001
Yeah, I've done some,
a little bit of soaring, but I'm not checked

00:06:08.001 --> 00:06:10.000
out in certain places.

00:06:10.001 --> 00:06:11.001
And now you have a bonanza?

00:06:11.001 --> 00:06:12.000
Mm-hmm.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.000
And how often do you fly now?

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.001
Well, very perversely, I bought it just about

00:06:19.001 --> 00:06:21.000
two months before the picture started.

00:06:21.001 --> 00:06:23.000
I decided to do a lot of flying.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.000
Now that's strange.

00:06:24.001 --> 00:06:27.001
You know, I've flown off and on the
last couple of years, but not a lot.

00:06:28.001 --> 00:06:31.000
Flying is kind of a thing where
you get into it and you get

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.001
out of it and you get back into it.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.001
The closer we got to doing this picture,
the more I was flying.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.000
I'm sure there's some
psychological reason for that.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.001
You weren't flying the
last time I spoke to you.

00:06:43.001 --> 00:06:45.001
Yeah, I've been flying since 64.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:46.001
Well, you have.

00:06:46.001 --> 00:06:48.001
That's when I didn't pay attention.

00:06:49.001 --> 00:06:50.001
You're not in the service?

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:51.000
No.

00:06:51.001 --> 00:06:51.001
No.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.000
I was doing Hawaii.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.001
And so that was the first year.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:06:59.001
Yeah.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:00.001
And then you came back where?

00:07:01.001 --> 00:07:04.000
I came back to Beverly Hills.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:05.001
Where you have another house?

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.000
Yeah, where we bought another house.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Where did you do that?

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:11.001
I don't know.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.001
The time seemed to...

00:07:13.001 --> 00:07:16.000
Look, give me an average day
for you in Beverly Hills.

00:07:16.001 --> 00:07:17.001
You get up with cars.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.000
I don't think I have an average day.

00:07:24.001 --> 00:07:26.000
I don't still live my life like that.

00:07:26.001 --> 00:07:27.000
Well, each day is different.

00:07:27.001 --> 00:07:29.000
You try to be, yeah.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.000
It's so radically different
when you have no job?

00:07:33.001 --> 00:07:35.000
Yeah, I drive around a lot, too.

00:07:36.001 --> 00:07:37.001
No, really, just give me a day.

00:07:37.001 --> 00:07:38.001
No, I'm telling you, I do.

00:07:38.001 --> 00:07:39.001
You drive, you get in your car.

00:07:39.001 --> 00:07:44.001
I get my car, I drive around, I think a lot,
and I drive out the beach, I drive

00:07:44.001 --> 00:07:48.000
down to San Diego, I drive to Santa Barbara,
out of the desert.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.000
Do you know where you're going
when you get in the car?

00:07:50.001 --> 00:07:51.000
You know.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:52.000
Mm-hmm.

00:07:52.001 --> 00:07:53.001
And what are you thinking about?

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.001
I guess, you know, just life, heavy.

00:08:00.001 --> 00:08:04.001
I don't know if I'm going to
get enough gas to get home.

00:08:05.001 --> 00:08:09.000
Well, now, you're independently
wealthy thanks to Superman, right?

00:08:10.001 --> 00:08:14.001
Yeah, well, I had money before Superman,
but I've done pretty well.

00:08:15.001 --> 00:08:17.000
So, actually, you don't have to come back?

00:08:17.001 --> 00:08:20.000
No,
I didn't come back because I needed the money.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:20.001
Right.

00:08:20.001 --> 00:08:23.000
Nor did I come back to, you know, to try to

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.000
rekindle any kind of start or anything.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:28.001
I just, that's really acting as really
the only thing I know how to do well.

00:08:32.001 --> 00:08:34.001
And I just thought I would come back to...

00:08:38.001 --> 00:08:42.000
Try to atone for some of the
things I had done earlier.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.000
Did this script grab you?

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:45.001
Yeah, it did.

00:08:45.001 --> 00:08:49.000
I like the idea of it because the
character changes quite dramatically.

00:08:50.001 --> 00:08:57.001
And I go from being, you know,
kind of a fairly uptight, middle management

00:08:57.001 --> 00:09:01.001
type guy to looking the way I am now, you know.

00:09:03.001 --> 00:09:05.001
You turned down a lot of scripts?

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.001
Yeah, I [...]

00:09:07.001 --> 00:09:13.000
In that two and a half years,
they constantly threw scripts at you?

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:18.000
No, I don't know constantly, but I would
say we turned down at least ten firm

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.001
offers for films.

00:09:20.001 --> 00:09:24.000
And I wouldn't even have considered,
you know, three quarters of them.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.001
Maybe there was two in there that
I would have maybe done if I had

00:09:27.001 --> 00:09:29.000
a mind to go back to work.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.000
Well, I still don't know what triggered
off your decision to go back to work.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.001
You say there were a couple of
scripts there you might have

00:09:36.001 --> 00:09:39.000
liked,
but at that time you weren't prepared for it.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:43.000
Yeah, well, early on there was a couple
of things that I kind of liked, but I

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.001
just really didn't feel like going back.

00:09:44.001 --> 00:09:48.000
Then I was offered a lot of adventure
films and that kind of thing.

00:09:48.001 --> 00:09:51.000
I felt, well, what's the sense in
coming back to do something like that?

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:58.001
Then this project had been
in the kind of works for the

00:09:58.001 --> 00:10:00.000
last nine or ten months.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.001
It was written for me,
and I liked the idea of it.

00:10:05.001 --> 00:10:08.000
So it took me that long to get it put together.

00:10:08.001 --> 00:10:10.001
You know, when you quit, you made some remarks

00:10:10.001 --> 00:10:12.001
critical of this business, haven't you?

00:10:12.001 --> 00:10:14.000
Oh, I would imagine, yeah.

00:10:14.001 --> 00:10:17.001
And you're one of the top box
office guys when you quit.

00:10:17.001 --> 00:10:20.001
You quit really almost on top, would you say?

00:10:20.001 --> 00:10:21.000
I don't know.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:24.001
Your career was, you could have gone on
and you got a big price for your movies.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.000
And yet you just decided
to quit because was it the

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.001
Superman thing that triggered it off?

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:33.000
That there was nothing wrong?

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.001
I think it was a combination of Superman
and March or Die, which was a film I had

00:10:38.001 --> 00:10:40.000
done just prior to that.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:46.000
I realized that after having done that,
that I was taking films for the money,

00:10:46.001 --> 00:10:49.001
not really looking at them that carefully.

00:10:50.001 --> 00:10:55.001
And then I knew that it wouldn't
be long before that would be over.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.000
You know, they would just sketch up with you.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:03.000
So I decided rather than being gradually,

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:09.001
gradually drifting down to things that I
didn't want to do, I would stop and just

00:11:09.001 --> 00:11:14.001
put it all aside and see if I couldn't
rekindle some kind of fire in me from the

00:11:14.001 --> 00:11:18.000
old days when I really loved
doing what I was doing.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:23.000
What was that movie you made the
one way to create the wire cap?

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:26.001
That didn't do too much, was it?

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:30.001
All the critics put that very
high among the old classics.

00:11:30.001 --> 00:11:32.000
Yeah, I like that work.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:35.000
You know,
I like that kind of intense kind of work.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:39.001
I mean, Scarecrow was done about the same time,
and I enjoyed that.

00:11:39.001 --> 00:11:40.000
And I...

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:41.001
French, good night, you good?

00:11:41.001 --> 00:11:42.000
Yeah.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:46.000
All kind of vital,
and it has to do with something.

00:11:46.001 --> 00:11:50.000
Well, were there scripts like
that coming along after that?

00:11:50.001 --> 00:11:55.000
There may have been, but I didn't see them,
or if I saw them, I didn't see them

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.000
as being that kind of...

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.001
You get seduced very easily in the business.
At least I do.

00:11:59.001 --> 00:12:05.001
I did. You know, you find that if you're
brought up in kind of poor circumstances

00:12:05.001 --> 00:12:12.001
that it's very easy to justify taking some
big money for something that you know

00:12:12.001 --> 00:12:14.001
deep down in you that is
not really great stuff.

00:12:15.001 --> 00:12:18.001
Well, now you've made a decision,
are you going to be very choosy?

00:12:18.001 --> 00:12:24.000
Not necessarily. I mean, I was choosy
about the first one to come back, but

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.000
I don't know.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:33.000
I think you go through a number of
stages in your life and in your

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.001
career about what you do.

00:12:35.001 --> 00:12:38.000
Was there things in your private life
that had any difficulties or such?

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:43.001
Oh, yeah. I've had a lot of difficulty
in my private life, and you take

00:12:43.001 --> 00:12:45.001
films for a number of reasons.

00:12:45.001 --> 00:12:46.001
You know, the location where somebody says,

00:12:46.001 --> 00:12:48.001
Morocco, oh, I've never been to Morocco.

00:12:49.001 --> 00:12:51.001
I don't think I have to read that script,
you know?

00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:01.000
People would be amazed at how many
movies have been done, you know, just

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:02.001
because a guy wants to get out of the house.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.000
And what's your marital status now?

00:13:08.001 --> 00:13:09.000
Pretty good.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.000
You're remarried, are you?

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:15.000
But you had problems.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:15.001
Twenty-four years.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:17.001
Yeah. There were problems there.

00:13:17.001 --> 00:13:20.001
Oh, yeah.
We've had difficulties, but we still care

00:13:20.001 --> 00:13:22.000
a great deal about each other.

00:13:22.001 --> 00:13:28.000
And we're fairly close,
considering, you know, all the

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:29.001
crap we've gone through.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.000
And you have children. How old are they?

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.000
They're 19's boy, Chris.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.000
Elizabeth is 17, and Leslie is 13.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:44.000
Are either of those children in the business?

00:13:44.001 --> 00:13:50.000
No, none of them, they have talked
just on the borders of wanting to,

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:55.001
you know, be in the business,
but none of them really want to work at it.

00:13:55.001 --> 00:14:00.001
So I haven't encouraged them. And by work
at it, I mean, they haven't talked in

00:14:00.001 --> 00:14:03.000
terms of going to New York and
studying and doing that kind of thing.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:07.001
So I certainly am not trying
to get them to work just to be

00:14:07.001 --> 00:14:09.001
movie people. You know, that's crazy.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:11.001
What does Chris want to do? Does he know yet?

00:14:12.001 --> 00:14:13.001
He's... Yeah, he knows.

00:14:15.001 --> 00:14:16.001
That doesn't do with the business.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:21.001
He wants to race. He races motorcycles,
has been for the last four or five years,

00:14:21.001 --> 00:14:23.001
and he's racing go-karts.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.000
He's going to go to England this summer.

00:14:26.001 --> 00:14:28.001
Really? How do you feel about it?
I know you're right.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.000
Well, yeah,
I can't very well tell him, you know,

00:14:31.001 --> 00:14:33.000
that's stupid because I do it myself.

00:14:33.001 --> 00:14:38.000
But I have tried to discourage him.
It's difficult, though.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:41.000
He wants to achieve something in his life.

00:14:42.001 --> 00:14:49.000
And so I don't know if he's going with
my blessing or with my hat on the back

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:52.000
or whatever, but I would prefer
to see him do something else.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.000
But if he's going to do it,
then he has to do it.

00:14:54.001 --> 00:14:57.000
He interviewed Larry Wilcox sometime back.
He was in a

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.001
race with me that you won, I believe.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:01.001
But was that the... Not the Jeep thing, was it?

00:15:01.001 --> 00:15:05.000
No, that was down at the Long Beach Grand Prix,
a celebrity race down there.

00:15:05.001 --> 00:15:06.000
That's right, yeah.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.001
Do you do a lot of that stuff? Yeah.

00:15:08.001 --> 00:15:11.000
You were in... Brolin, I guess?

00:15:11.001 --> 00:15:13.000
Brolin was in the race, yeah.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:15.001
I don't know if Clint was in that race or not.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:16.000
I kind of remember.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.001
Newman was in a race we
did back at Watkins Glen.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.001
What kick do you get out of racing?

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:30.000
Well, it's interesting to...

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:34.000
It has not to do with speed so much.
It has to do with control.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:44.001
How much control can you have if you
take a car in a certain area that you've

00:15:44.001 --> 00:15:47.000
been over 15, 20, 30 times?

00:15:47.001 --> 00:15:50.000
It's nothing like that on the freeway,
diving in

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:51.001
and out,
and you don't know what's going to happen.

00:15:52.001 --> 00:15:55.001
When you're racing, it has to do with
having gone around the track a number of

00:15:55.001 --> 00:15:58.000
times and knowing what gear you're
going to be in and how much braking you

00:15:58.000 --> 00:15:59.001
have to do to get through a corner.

00:15:59.001 --> 00:16:06.000
The fun of it for me, and maybe different
for other people, is seeing how much

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:11.000
control you have over that vehicle through
a certain corner at a certain speed.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:13.000
So it's really very skillful.

00:16:13.001 --> 00:16:16.001
It has to do with skill as
opposed to courage so much.

00:16:17.001 --> 00:16:21.001
You can't be totally chicken,
but you don't need as much

00:16:21.001 --> 00:16:23.000
courage as people think you do.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.001
At least at the level I'm doing that.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.000
If you're talking about Indianapolis or
something else, that's something that...

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:29.001
Have you ever thought about Indianapolis?

00:16:30.001 --> 00:16:32.001
I was brought up around 80 miles from there. I

00:16:32.001 --> 00:16:34.000
saw all the races when I was a kid.

00:16:34.001 --> 00:16:37.000
So that's always been kind of a...

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:42.000
I had this mystique about it for me.
I don't want to do it at this age.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:44.000
No? Is that...

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.000
Well, you have to re-skill number one.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:51.001
It takes years and years to get the kind
of skill, unless you're a young person.

00:16:51.001 --> 00:16:56.001
I guess Steve McLean [...]

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:00.000
He was trying to prove something to himself.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:04.001
To go racing like that at high speeds.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:07.000
But that has...

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:11.001
Well, it could be. I don't know.
I've never gotten into it that heavily

00:17:11.001 --> 00:17:13.001
in terms of...

00:17:14.001 --> 00:17:16.000
He was quite good at it.

00:17:16.001 --> 00:17:18.000
What's the fastest you've ever gone?

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:21.000
About 160, I suppose.

00:17:22.001 --> 00:17:24.000
Have you ever been in a serious accident?

00:17:29.001 --> 00:17:32.001
You were a trifle heavier than when I last...

00:17:32.001 --> 00:17:33.000
Really?

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:33.001
[...]

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:34.001
Oh, that's nice.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.000
Had to come down here today to tell me that.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:40.001
Do you really...
Do you think you're at your same weight?

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:41.001
Oh, I have no idea.

00:17:42.001 --> 00:17:45.001
No, I'm sure you're... I must have been
doing Poseidon or something like that.

00:17:46.001 --> 00:17:47.001
That's fairly thin.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.001
That's right. That's exactly right.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:51.000
I don't know that I remember it all that.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.000
What did you think of that,
appearing in something like that?

00:17:57.001 --> 00:18:01.000
Well, at the time it seemed fine, you know,
because I was just coming off French

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:04.000
Connection, and I had three other pictures
to do afterwards, and it was, you

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:07.001
know, grab it and run, and it seemed okay.
It was the first studio

00:18:07.001 --> 00:18:09.000
picture I'd ever...

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:09.001
[...] waiting time.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.000
You know, the first time I'd ever
worked in a studio in the last.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.001
It was interesting.

00:18:15.001 --> 00:18:17.001
I didn't think the picture was great,
as far as I was concerned.

00:18:18.001 --> 00:18:21.001
It was... It worked on the level
that it was supposed to work.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.000
Well, I'm trying to understand your motivation.

00:18:25.001 --> 00:18:29.000
You came back because you now suddenly...

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:30.001
You understand that when you explain it to me.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:33.000
Well,
I think you're trying to prove something again.

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:41.001
I mean, this is the way I get it,
that you want to become recognized as

00:18:41.001 --> 00:18:46.000
a fine actor, rather than a guy who's
just grabbing the money and running.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.000
Well, I don't know about the recognition.
Yeah, you may be right about that.

00:18:51.001 --> 00:18:55.000
It goes hand in hand in hand.
Certainly, if you're

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:56.001
doing good work, you're going to be recognized.

00:18:59.001 --> 00:19:01.000
It's mostly for myself.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:09.001
I just... I feel that if I can
do good work and satisfy myself,

00:19:09.001 --> 00:19:11.000
that that's all that's really necessary.

00:19:12.001 --> 00:19:15.001
Is it tougher for you now to come
back after two and a half years as an

00:19:15.001 --> 00:19:17.000
actor who hasn't used it for two years?

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:19.000
No. It comes right back.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:21.000
It's like bicycle or something?
Is that the same?

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:23.000
Swimming or some of the other things.

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:25.001
It goes right into it. You don't have to do...

00:19:25.001 --> 00:19:29.001
I think if I had to go back on stage,
it would be different.

00:19:29.001 --> 00:19:30.000
[...]

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.001
You know, the Broadway stage.

00:19:35.001 --> 00:19:36.000
Because...

00:19:36.001 --> 00:19:38.000
It takes some special skills.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.000
What is it that you like about acting?

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:45.001
I guess the concentration.

00:19:46.001 --> 00:19:47.001
When you're actually doing it.

00:19:47.001 --> 00:19:51.001
The enormous concentration
that really fills you up.

00:19:52.001 --> 00:19:58.000
That you can tell when you're doing it right.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:19:59.001
You know.

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:06.000
And you get that wonderful feedback from

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:08.000
where that comes from in your body.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:09.000
In your mind.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:14.000
That tells you that you're doing
something that's right on.

00:20:14.001 --> 00:20:15.001
You feel it with the act.

00:20:17.001 --> 00:20:23.000
I guess the real problem with
actors is keeping that mechanism...

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:31.000
honed to the point where you don't
start getting outside stimuli.

00:20:36.001 --> 00:20:38.000
Where you...

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:41.001
You then allow that to affect your work.

00:20:42.001 --> 00:20:44.001
Whether negative or positive.

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:52.000
That you have to somehow rely on that...
whatever that feeling is.

00:20:52.001 --> 00:20:55.001
Was there ever a point where you
thought you might never come back?

00:20:56.001 --> 00:20:57.000
Yeah.

00:20:59.001 --> 00:21:01.000
Five minutes or so.

00:21:02.001 --> 00:21:04.001
No, I go through it every day.

00:21:04.001 --> 00:21:05.000
[...] of it.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:06.001
[...] oh, how?

00:21:06.001 --> 00:21:07.000
You think you made a mistake?

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:08.000
What am I doing this for?

00:21:08.001 --> 00:21:08.001
[...]

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:12.000
Where you say you made a mistake coming back?

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:13.001
Oh, I don't know.

00:21:13.001 --> 00:21:14.001
The regret, I mean.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:15.001
I don't know.

00:21:18.001 --> 00:21:22.001
I don't regret having come back
for this film because much of this

00:21:22.001 --> 00:21:24.000
is enjoyable to me.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:25.001
What would you have done
if you didn't come back?

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:27.000
Say you...

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.001
Well, I paint a little and I do some...

00:21:29.001 --> 00:21:32.000
I just start working in stone,
which I really love.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:33.000
Stone?

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:34.001
Yeah, marble.

00:21:35.001 --> 00:21:36.000
Sculpting.

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:36.001
Oh, really?

00:21:38.001 --> 00:21:44.000
Ideally, I see myself someplace looking
over a meadow with a lot of trees,

00:21:44.001 --> 00:21:48.001
looking down to the ocean in the
studio where I am carving stone.

00:21:51.001 --> 00:21:54.000
An hour later, I don't know what I'm gonna do.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:56.000
You want to get in the car and go to San Diego?

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:56.000
Yeah.

00:21:56.001 --> 00:21:58.000
I have to drive out to...

00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:59.000
But you can afford to do that, right?

00:21:59.000 --> 00:21:59.001
City and industry.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:00.001
You can do that.

00:22:00.001 --> 00:22:03.000
You can get up on top of a
hill and do your marble.

00:22:03.001 --> 00:22:04.001
You don't have to work.

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:08.000
You say you see yourself.

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:11.000
Yeah, no,
I see myself because I like the idea there.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:13.000
I like the fantasy of that.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:14.001
Whether or not I'll ever really do that.

00:22:14.001 --> 00:22:15.000
I don't know.

00:22:16.001 --> 00:22:18.000
You need something to occupy your mind.

00:22:18.001 --> 00:22:18.001
Yeah, I do.

00:22:20.001 --> 00:22:23.001
And it wasn't hard for two and a
half years not to work at all.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:25.000
You didn't do anything?

00:22:26.001 --> 00:22:28.000
Well, you know, I've been asked that a lot.

00:22:28.001 --> 00:22:30.001
And I could never come up with
any answer other than they.

00:22:30.001 --> 00:22:33.000
I read a lot and I've painted
a little and I flew a little.

00:22:33.001 --> 00:22:35.000
And outside of that, I can't tell you.

00:22:35.001 --> 00:22:36.000
But you didn't work there?

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:41.000
No, well, I worked two days for
Warren Beatty on Reds in London.

00:22:41.001 --> 00:22:42.001
Why did you do that?

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:43.001
Oh, just as a favor?

00:22:43.001 --> 00:22:45.001
Yeah. No billing, no money.

00:22:45.001 --> 00:22:46.001
Did you travel a lot?

00:22:48.001 --> 00:22:49.000
Traveled some, you know.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.000
We traveled quite a bit last summer, actually.

00:22:51.001 --> 00:22:52.001
Well, what did your wife say?

00:22:52.001 --> 00:22:53.001
[...] too home every day.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:55.001
Good night.

00:22:57.001 --> 00:22:59.001
[...] worst one you can think of?

00:22:59.001 --> 00:23:00.000
What's the worst one you've ever thought of?

00:23:00.001 --> 00:23:02.000
Well, it was kind of a treat, though.

00:23:02.001 --> 00:23:05.000
I mean, I've never had a treat for her.

00:23:06.001 --> 00:23:08.001
A modesty [...]

00:23:09.001 --> 00:23:12.001
Uh, God, because I hadn't been home
for about ten years, you know.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:13.001
She said, hello.

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:17.000
So you got to know each other?

00:23:17.001 --> 00:23:17.001
Yeah.

00:23:18.001 --> 00:23:19.000
And your children?

00:23:23.001 --> 00:23:24.001
That was a delightful time.

00:23:25.001 --> 00:23:26.000
Yeah, it was.

00:23:26.001 --> 00:23:28.000
You don't regret that you didn't.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:28.001
No, no.

00:23:28.001 --> 00:23:30.000
That was a good period, though.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:30.000
Yeah.

00:23:30.001 --> 00:23:31.001
You did a lot of heavy thinking.

00:23:32.001 --> 00:23:34.000
And now you've got a lot of things resolved.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:35.000
No.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:40.000
But I decided that it can't be resolved,
you know.

00:23:40.001 --> 00:23:43.001
There are some things that
you just can't resolve.

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:50.001
I guess making a decision, the decision
that this is going to be great working

00:23:50.001 --> 00:23:52.000
in movies at this time.

00:23:52.001 --> 00:23:55.000
It would be a wrong decision,
because I know it's not going to be

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:56.001
great, but it's all I know how to do.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:23:59.000
Are you looking past this picture?

00:23:59.001 --> 00:23:59.001
No.

00:24:01.001 --> 00:24:03.000
Are you a guy that looks ahead and...

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:04.000
I don't like to look ahead.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:07.001
One of the most depressing times for me
was when I first came out from New York

00:24:07.001 --> 00:24:13.000
after Bonnie and Clyde,
and before it was released.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:17.001
And the agent out here had lined
up three films for me to do.

00:24:17.001 --> 00:24:22.001
I wasn't starting anything there,
but worked for a whole year.

00:24:23.001 --> 00:24:28.000
And I found myself up at the Montecito Hotel,
sitting

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:29.001
there waiting, ready to go to work one day.

00:24:29.001 --> 00:24:31.001
I was so depressed, I couldn't figure out why.

00:24:32.001 --> 00:24:38.000
And I finally figured that I had,
for the previous 15 years in New York, I had

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:43.001
never known more than two weeks'
work in advance at the most.

00:24:44.001 --> 00:24:49.000
And doing theater had always been, well,
you're on a five day when you first

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:50.001
start rehearsal on a theater.

00:24:51.001 --> 00:24:53.001
They can fire you any time
within the first five

00:24:53.001 --> 00:24:55.000
days, and then they have to give you two weeks'
notice.

00:24:57.001 --> 00:25:01.000
So I had never known anything other than that,
and I kind of liked it.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:04.000
You know, living on the brink kind of thing.

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:08.000
I mean, it wasn't terribly dangerous
other than with the job, but I had

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:09.001
been in New York since 1953.

00:25:13.001 --> 00:25:18.000
It had been kind of relaxed and easy,
you know, and then I had

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:19.001
this work to look forward to.

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:21.001
And granted, it was things I wanted to do.

00:25:22.001 --> 00:25:25.000
I mean, I wanted to act.
It wasn't necessarily great films.

00:25:27.001 --> 00:25:31.001
So it's nice not to have more
than one film to do at a time.

00:25:33.001 --> 00:25:35.001
Suppose this picture hadn't come along.

00:25:37.001 --> 00:25:39.000
Something would have come along, I think.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:41.000
I mean, you had made a decision,
you were going back.

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:45.001
Yeah. I had decided about eight,
nine months ago that I was time to go

00:25:45.001 --> 00:25:47.001
back,
and I started seriously looking for things.

00:25:47.001 --> 00:25:49.001
You called your agent and said, yeah.

00:25:50.001 --> 00:25:54.000
I'm looking for stuff.
Was there something in particular you wanted?

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:57.001
I had never really told anybody that I
wasn't going to work, that I was finished.

00:25:57.001 --> 00:25:59.001
I just said, hey,
I'd like to take some time off.

00:26:00.001 --> 00:26:02.001
And people would call every month or so

00:26:02.001 --> 00:26:04.000
and say, hey, there's such and such a script.

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:06.000
And I'd say, nah, what do you think?

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:08.001
And they'd say, well,
if you're going to come back for

00:26:08.001 --> 00:26:10.001
something, you know,
let's wait and let's hold out.

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:14.001
So it wasn't as if I'd
announced my retirement at all.

00:26:14.001 --> 00:26:19.001
I hate those facts that I
retire in every five minutes.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:20.001
And how did I leave?

00:26:21.001 --> 00:26:22.001
There's an office.

00:26:24.001 --> 00:26:25.001
Scott's retired at all.

00:26:28.001 --> 00:26:34.000
Was there something, especially,
there was some kind of character you wanted?

00:26:34.001 --> 00:26:35.000
Well, I wanted to play something

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:37.000
lighter.
I didn't care so much about the character.

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:40.001
I never looked at things for character.
I just wanted to lighten up a little.

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:46.000
I just felt that I was being so heavy and
it was kind of dominating my life that

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:49.001
I ended up...

00:26:49.001 --> 00:26:55.001
People thought of me as this kind
of very hard guy or ugly, you know.

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.000
I guess that's the better word for it.

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:00.001
And I didn't want to be ugly all the time.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:04.000
You didn't play any, you have never
played any comedy or anything like that?

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:05.000
Only on Broadway.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.000
Have you any desire to go back to Broadway?

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:08.001
Yeah, I do.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:10.000
You do want to go back?

00:27:10.001 --> 00:27:11.000
Yeah, I do go back.

00:27:12.001 --> 00:27:15.000
Can you put that in there so they
can start sending me things?

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:21.000
During the two and a half year hiatus,
whatever we want to call

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:22.001
it, did you ever think of Broadway back then?

00:27:22.001 --> 00:27:26.001
Well, what I actually had planned to
do and it didn't work out was, when I

00:27:26.001 --> 00:27:30.000
finished Superman,
we had decided to move up to Pebble Beach.

00:27:30.001 --> 00:27:34.001
And I wanted to build a theater in Monterey,
or somewhere in that area.

00:27:34.001 --> 00:27:38.001
And I found out after getting up there
that there were several theaters and it

00:27:38.001 --> 00:27:45.001
was a very tough go because it's a little
touristy in the summertime and not

00:27:45.001 --> 00:27:48.001
enough in the wintertime
to keep the theater alive.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.001
And I wanted to do kind of a
repertoire with a permanent company.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:57.001
I may eventually still do that someday
if I can find a spot to do it.

00:27:58.001 --> 00:27:59.000
You know Brent Reynolds?

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:01.001
And you know about his...

00:28:01.001 --> 00:28:03.000
Yeah, I was down there a couple months ago.

00:28:04.001 --> 00:28:05.001
It's a great place, huh?

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:07.001
Yeah, it works quite well for him too.

00:28:08.001 --> 00:28:10.001
The problem with dinner theater though is that

00:28:10.001 --> 00:28:12.001
it's kind of a compromise, I think.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:19.000
It may not be as much a compromise as film is,
but it is some kind of a...

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:20.000
It's got to be commercial.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:23.001
Yeah,
you have to do commercial projects and many of

00:28:23.001 --> 00:28:25.000
them are kind of restatements.

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:34.001
You've maybe already done the project or you...

00:28:34.001 --> 00:28:36.000
[...]

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:43.000
And I thought maybe to do some
experimental theater or to do some classic

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:48.001
American classics, Williams and Eugene O'Neill

00:28:48.001 --> 00:28:50.001
and that kind of thing would be interesting.

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.000
What about... Do you know Barbara Streisand?

00:28:57.001 --> 00:28:58.000
Mm-hmm.

00:28:58.001 --> 00:28:59.001
Because of the same age?

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:00.001
Yeah.

00:29:01.001 --> 00:29:03.000
[...] mean, do you know her well?

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:06.001
Well, I've gotten to know her in
the last couple weeks pretty well.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:08.000
But before that?

00:29:08.001 --> 00:29:13.000
Well, I had not only met her at parties
and once up in Pebble Beach, she was up

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:19.000
years ago when she was at Sue's wedding.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:22.000
Actually Sue and Joan Claude
were married in Pebble Beach.

00:29:22.001 --> 00:29:24.001
So we knew each other a little bit.

00:29:25.001 --> 00:29:27.000
And you have never performed before?

00:29:29.001 --> 00:29:31.000
No, she doesn't work until next week.

00:29:31.001 --> 00:29:32.001
You're looking forward to that.

00:29:34.001 --> 00:29:34.001
Yeah.

00:29:35.001 --> 00:29:38.001
I can see from the rehearsal that she's
got a lot of juice and a lot of...

00:29:39.001 --> 00:29:40.001
It's going to be kind of fun.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:42.001
That's interesting.

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:44.001
Interesting.

00:29:54.001 --> 00:29:59.000
Well, I've asked through the
things that I had on my mind.

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:04.000
And you're going to perform today?

00:30:05.001 --> 00:30:08.000
Well, I guess you'd call it that.

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:13.000
Sometimes I come to work and I think,
I'll just walk around today

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:14.001
and not give them too much.

00:30:16.001 --> 00:30:18.001
I always try to do the best I can.

00:30:20.001 --> 00:30:24.000
I was making a joke about performance,
but some things

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:26.000
are not really a performance.

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:29.000
I mean, because I was just thinking
about what I just did up there.

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:30.001
I just walked through a room.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:34.001
Well, you could walk through a room and
try to make a performance out of it, but

00:30:34.001 --> 00:30:36.000
it might be wrong for the film.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:43.000
Or you could do something there that
might necessarily not be pertinent.

00:30:44.001 --> 00:30:47.000
It might be interesting at
the time and interesting

00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:48.001
in the dailies, but it may not be...

00:30:49.001 --> 00:30:50.001
Hey, Kevin.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:51.001
How you doing?

00:30:53.001 --> 00:30:54.000
Good to see you.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:57.001
You going to watch this performer?

00:30:58.001 --> 00:31:00.000
Well, I've got to go for a physical.

00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:02.000
I'm not the friend of yours, though.

00:31:02.001 --> 00:31:03.001
Just recently, shoot it for me.

00:31:04.001 --> 00:31:05.000
Oh, yeah.

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:07.001
Oh, he's a hell of an actor.

00:31:08.001 --> 00:31:12.000
Have you ever had experience...
I'm doing a separate story.

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:15.000
Have you ever experienced problems
with another actor on the set?

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:18.000
Physical problems?

00:31:18.001 --> 00:31:21.000
No,
just where you just couldn't get along or...

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:24.001
Oh, yeah. ...temperamental things and...

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:29.000
Yeah, sure, I think everybody has...
...personnelies or whatever.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:34.000
Is it very difficult working
on those conditions?

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:41.000
Yeah, I worked with Sandy Dennis
on stage for almost a year.

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:48.000
In the last... ...the middle six months
of that, we never spoke to each other.

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:49.001
I think it would be on stage, yeah.

00:31:49.001 --> 00:31:50.000
Which point was that?

00:31:50.001 --> 00:31:51.000
Any Wednesday.

00:31:53.001 --> 00:31:59.000
And it was just misunderstandings
and things like that.

00:32:01.001 --> 00:32:03.000
I often wonder how people can do it.

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:09.000
You know, act... ...things are going
so great and I [...] seeing breaks.

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:12.001
So that is act...
to give the greatest test I can.

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:16.001
We were talking the other day about this.

00:32:16.001 --> 00:32:20.001
It has not to do with not getting along,
but in that play, I came... ...went on

00:32:20.001 --> 00:32:25.000
one night... with a girl who I had never met.

00:32:25.001 --> 00:32:27.001
Sandy had gotten sick at the last minute.

00:32:29.001 --> 00:32:33.001
And I guess they forgot to tell me that
she wasn't going to be there, but I came

00:32:33.001 --> 00:32:35.000
on about five minutes after the play starts.

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:39.000
And just before I went on stage,
the stage manager said, Oh, by the

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:41.001
way, Sandy's not there.

00:32:41.001 --> 00:32:42.000
And I said, Who is it?

00:32:42.001 --> 00:32:44.001
And they had just replaced the understudy who I

00:32:44.001 --> 00:32:46.000
had worked with a couple of times.

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:48.001
And they said, It's Diane, somebody.

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:55.000
And I went on seeing this person who I
had never seen before, which was okay for

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:58.000
the early part of the play because
I wasn't supposed to know her.

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:02.001
But then like ten minutes later,
half an hour later, we were doing a love scene

00:33:02.001 --> 00:33:04.000
on the couch here in this...

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:04.001
Hello!

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:09.000
And the poor thing,
she was just frightened to death.

00:33:09.001 --> 00:33:12.001
She had been thrown on at
the last second literally.

00:33:13.001 --> 00:33:15.001
Too big fun if you were in a crowd scene,
you didn't know which one she was.

00:33:19.001 --> 00:33:22.001
You can choose your own director.
You have that in your contract?

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:26.001
Well,
it depends on how the thing is put together.

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:30.000
Many times, the director is the
first one put into a project.

00:33:30.001 --> 00:33:33.001
The more the director finds the project.

00:33:34.001 --> 00:33:37.001
But if it's certainly a project of my own,
I would choose my own director.

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:41.000
Well, where have you picked your own director?

00:33:42.001 --> 00:33:46.000
Actually, the only time I've ever picked
my own director is John Frankenheimer

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:49.000
in French Connection 2.

00:33:52.001 --> 00:33:55.000
Are there certain directors
you would like to work with?

00:33:55.001 --> 00:33:58.000
Oh, there's a lot of good directors around now.

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:01.001
[...] piece of good,
fine director, a minister for him.

00:34:03.001 --> 00:34:05.001
How important do you think
that is for an actor?

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:06.001
Well, I think it's everything.

00:34:08.001 --> 00:34:14.000
It's 75% of filmmaking,
I'd say probably 25% is the story.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:15.001
[...] Well, really?

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:20.000
I use a piece of meat, and I try to
walk around in front of the camera.

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:21.000
You sound like Hitchcock.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:23.001
I don't really think that.

00:34:24.001 --> 00:34:27.001
The director is probably 40%
in the script, and 40 CAMERON.

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:31.000
And the actors are almost...

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:33.000
I think the actor is only about 20%, really.

00:34:33.001 --> 00:34:34.000
But you think so.

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:39.001
You take ten top actors,
and most of them could play the same role.

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:45.001
It's been trained to various degrees
of being accepted depending on their

00:34:45.001 --> 00:34:48.001
casting, their body types,
and what they look like.

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:53.001
While you were watching the movies over
the last two and a half years, which I

00:34:53.001 --> 00:34:59.000
imagine did, the film,
did you see anything there where you said...

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:03.000
I liked to have been in China Syndrome.
I liked that quite well.

00:35:07.001 --> 00:35:09.000
Playing the Douglas part?

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:11.001
No, I couldn't have played that.

00:35:12.001 --> 00:35:14.000
Maybe the role Jack Lennon did.

00:35:14.001 --> 00:35:16.001
Not that he didn't do it well,
I thought he did it wonderfully well.

00:35:19.001 --> 00:35:21.000
There's a number of things that I saw that I

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:22.001
thought, wow, that would have been nice to do.

00:35:22.001 --> 00:35:29.001
But all actors see things differently,
so you don't necessarily go to a film

00:35:29.001 --> 00:35:32.000
and see, ah, he didn't do it right.

00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:35.001
I might have made other choices or something.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:41.000
Is your attitude now toward this film
the same as it was when you were doing

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:43.001
something like a conversation?

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:50.000
Do you still have that same
drive and that same...

00:35:50.000 --> 00:35:52.001
No, I think that that will never be the same.

00:35:52.001 --> 00:35:57.000
Yeah, I think that was like
the first and it can never be.

00:35:57.000 --> 00:35:58.000
Five-minute warning.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:04.000
I mean, if you're going to score a touchdown,
you're going to feel cold.

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:05.001
Are we going to...

00:36:05.001 --> 00:36:07.001
[...] to a sudden victory.

00:36:08.001 --> 00:36:15.000
Yeah, I think that one tends
to expel whatever you have to

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:19.000
offer in maybe ten or fifteen films,
and then there's restatements of things

00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:24.001
you've done in various collages of,
you know, ways, trick ways or whatever.

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:27.000
I'm just trying to get about your enthusiasm.

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:30.000
Oh, how high you are.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.001
Business tends to kill a
lot of your enthusiasm.

00:36:35.001 --> 00:36:38.001
Well, I mean, does that depend on the project,
though? I mean, to say this...

00:36:38.001 --> 00:36:43.001
Well, I think it depends on a lot of things.
It depends a lot on the director.

00:36:43.001 --> 00:36:49.000
You know, the director really is the father.
He's the artistic person that you

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:54.000
one should look to.
He's the guiding force in any film.

00:36:54.001 --> 00:36:56.001
How about directing yourself? Have you ever...

00:36:56.001 --> 00:36:57.001
I think it would be wonderful.

00:36:59.000 --> 00:37:02.000
No, not directing yourself,
but have you ever...

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:08.001
No, I've thought a lot about directing.
And I think actors probably fall into the

00:37:08.001 --> 00:37:15.000
trap of thinking that directing is maybe
easier than... it actually is, because

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:19.001
you get on the set and you start seeing
things with a fresh eye, where the

00:37:19.001 --> 00:37:24.001
director and his people have been involved
in it for nine months, you know, and

00:37:24.001 --> 00:37:30.000
they have these ideas, and you come on and say,
Oh, let's move that wall over

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:34.001
there. I feel, you know, you just have
this kind of fresh... And you can't do

00:37:34.001 --> 00:37:36.000
that. You just... I mean, the business...

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:37.000
But you make suggestions.

00:37:37.001 --> 00:37:38.000
Yeah.

00:37:38.001 --> 00:37:41.001
Where you think it could be improved, or...

00:37:41.001 --> 00:37:43.000
That's touchy sometimes, isn't it?

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:45.001
Yeah, it gets tricky,
but there again, it depends

00:37:45.001 --> 00:37:47.000
on the personality you're working with.

00:37:47.001 --> 00:37:53.000
Some directors make you think that the
whole thing is you. It all has to do with

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:57.001
your ideas, and you know, more than
likely that's the one director who has

00:37:57.001 --> 00:38:00.000
thought it all out,
and you end up doing exactly what he wants.

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:04.001
But actors are children in many ways, you know.
They just

00:38:04.001 --> 00:38:06.001
like to have their own way.

00:38:06.001 --> 00:38:13.000
[...] the couple is like that in a way.
I mean, you know that there's really a

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:19.001
strong force there in somebody guiding
this thing, and you're willing and

00:38:19.001 --> 00:38:22.000
encouraged to make all kinds of suggestions.

00:38:22.001 --> 00:38:26.001
And they kind of all filter in there,
and they all kind of come back in some way.

00:38:27.001 --> 00:38:29.001
And you really feel a part of it.

00:38:30.001 --> 00:38:33.001
All these actors he's ever worked
with are very loyal to you.

00:38:36.001 --> 00:38:39.000
Could you have made a living at
something else that you didn't do?

00:38:39.001 --> 00:38:42.000
Oh, yeah, sure. I drove a truck pretty good.

00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:50.001
I don't know if I'd ever been successful
at anything that was terribly

00:38:50.001 --> 00:38:55.000
regimented, or because I had
terrible difficulty in the service.

00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:58.001
I was in for five years and made PFC.

00:38:59.001 --> 00:39:02.000
I remember you told me you banged up your knee,
too, didn't you?

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:02.001
Yeah.

00:39:05.001 --> 00:39:09.001
I think I've had an operation on it since then.
This is my third operation on the

00:39:09.001 --> 00:39:12.000
knee, so it'll never be much good.

00:39:14.001 --> 00:39:15.001
Coach, I want to thank you.

00:39:15.001 --> 00:39:16.000
Thank you.

00:39:18.001 --> 00:39:25.000
These masters, they're all supposed to be...
it's not in our class. They're mass

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:27.001
producing, genuine oil paintings.

00:39:27.001 --> 00:39:28.000
Is it?

00:39:28.001 --> 00:39:29.001
I don't know.

00:39:29.001 --> 00:39:35.000
And there's kind of a story point about it,
since she doesn't want to live in the

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:37.001
loft next to all those Koreans or something.

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:44.000
That sounds a little risky to me,
but I guess there's not that much of

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:45.001
a Korean audience in the United States.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:47.000
You paint yourself?

00:39:47.001 --> 00:39:50.000
A little bit, but I like to sculpt.

00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:55.001
Have you sold anything,
or you do just for your own...

00:39:55.001 --> 00:39:56.001
I don't know. I've been letting my seat.

00:39:56.001 --> 00:39:57.001
What do you mean, salad?

00:40:00.001 --> 00:40:03.000
What have you done?
What kind of stuff do you do?

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:08.000
Well, I just started...the first thing
I did was a piece on soap stones, this

00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:10.001
kind of green, very soft rock.

00:40:12.000 --> 00:40:18.001
[...] kind of a Burmese lady
face with a pair in the hand.

00:40:19.001 --> 00:40:20.001
And it was fun.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:23.000
And then I did my own hand.

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:25.000
[...]

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:32.000
Now I'm doing a...
I bought a 200 block of marble.

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:34.001
And I'm going in to get a face out.

00:40:35.001 --> 00:40:36.001
It's that hard work.

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:39.001
Did you take any instruction
you were doing this morning?

00:40:39.001 --> 00:40:42.000
Well,
I studied at the Art Students League years ago.

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:42.001
Oh, that's right, sure.

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:44.000
In New York.

00:40:46.001 --> 00:40:47.001
See you...

00:40:53.000 --> 00:40:55.000
This is your first American movie?

00:40:57.000 --> 00:41:00.000
I wouldn't tell it as long ago
before it was a good, awesome [...]

00:41:04.000 --> 00:41:07.001
Have you wanted to do one before this?

00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:08.001
How about this?

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:13.001
You and Hackman are going
to make a great thing.

00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:15.000
A great one line.

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:18.000
[...]

00:41:20.001 --> 00:41:24.001
And I think you really need to want
to do it desperately to get it on.

00:41:25.001 --> 00:41:26.000
Unless you do...

00:41:29.000 --> 00:41:30.001
[...] mean, material is a little different.

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:37.000
I must say I'm lucky because this
project only took two years.

00:41:37.000 --> 00:41:41.000
It's been exactly two years to today
that I went to Seattle and I had joined.

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:44.001
It was a fox at that time with an idea.

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:52.000
And then a year left between the idea and
getting a screenwriter and writing it.

00:41:53.000 --> 00:41:55.000
Another year to get it on.

00:41:55.001 --> 00:41:59.000
Did you see Hackman in the role originally?

00:41:59.001 --> 00:42:00.000
Frankly.

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:03.001
We wrote it for...

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:05.000
Except you didn't know it.

00:42:05.001 --> 00:42:05.001
Really?

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:06.000
Yeah.

00:42:06.001 --> 00:42:09.000
And it just took a chance because in his...

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:15.001
To me, he's probably the best
actor on the screen today.

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:17.001
And certainly in his age group, he has no fear.

00:42:18.001 --> 00:42:22.000
Weren't you a little worried that he might
not accept as he had all the other...

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:23.000
He didn't have to roll the dice.

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:27.000
He's been refusing everything.
He refused everything last one hour.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:30.001
[...]

00:42:30.001 --> 00:42:31.000
You really...

00:42:32.001 --> 00:42:33.001
I think you have a big chance.

00:42:33.001 --> 00:42:35.000
Did you try to persuade him yourself?

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:39.000
No, I said in the script, the fact that
I knew him made it even more awkward.

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:43.000
Because for two weeks he didn't read it.
He was too afraid. He didn't hate it.

00:42:43.001 --> 00:42:44.001
I was sweating bullets.

00:42:46.001 --> 00:42:49.000
And finally when he read it, like an hour
and a half later he called and said,

